Reflection on Round 12 Optimisation Strategy
Over the course of the earlier rounds, my optimisation strategy has evolved from a largely exploratory and random approach to a more structured and data-driven process. In the first few rounds, my queries were mostly distributed broadly across the search space in order to understand how the unknown functions behaved. This was useful for gathering initial information, but it often resulted in inefficient sampling because many queries were placed far from promising regions. As more data points were collected, my approach gradually became more systematic. I began analysing patterns in previous results, identifying areas where higher-performing values were located, and placing new queries closer to those regions while still allowing some exploration to avoid missing better solutions elsewhere.

With 21 data points now available, the search space can be viewed as a higher-dimensional structure where certain variables appear to influence the outcomes more strongly than others. Some dimensions show large changes in the function values when slightly adjusted, while others appear to have relatively small effects. These high-impact variables behave similarly to principal components in PCA, where most of the variation in the results can be explained by a smaller number of dominant directions. Identifying these influential variables helps focus the search on directions where improvements are most likely.

To decide which aspects of the strategy to keep exploring versus simplifying, I look at consistency and variation in previous results. If a particular region of the search space repeatedly produces strong outcomes, I concentrate queries around that region to refine the estimate of the optimum. At the same time, I maintain a smaller level of exploration in other regions to ensure the algorithm does not prematurely converge on a local optimum. This process is similar to how PCA reduces dimensionality while retaining the most informative variance—less informative directions can be sampled less frequently, while important ones receive more attention.

This round of optimisation will strongly influence my approach to the final round in Module 24. With a relatively large dataset already available, the next step will likely focus more heavily on exploitation, meaning I will place queries closer to the most promising points discovered so far. However, I will still keep a limited level of exploration to confirm whether nearby regions might contain better solutions. The goal will be to balance refining the best candidate solutions with avoiding overconfidence in potentially suboptimal areas.

Insights from PCA also influence how I interpret the results of the BBO process. By focusing on patterns of variance across the dataset, I can identify which parts of the search space contribute meaningful information and which appear redundant. This perspective encourages a more efficient search strategy by prioritising informative directions while reducing unnecessary randomness in the query selection process.
